+# Background processes
+
+Long-lived commands for a coding-agent session: dev servers, test watchers, local APIs, and slow builds. Each command runs in its own process group, does not inherit pi's stdio, and can wake the agent instead of the agent polling.
+
+```bash
+pi -e packages/coding-agent/examples/extensions/background-processes
+```
+
+Or copy this directory to `~/.pi/agent/extensions/background-processes/` (DefaultResourceLoader picks up `index.ts`).
+
+## Tools
+
+| Tool | Purpose |
+|------|---------|
+| `bg_run` | Start a command in the background and return immediately with its process record |
+| `bg_logs` | Page combined stdout/stderr (newest lines by default, never more than 64KB) |
+| `bg_list` | List every process visible to this session, running and finished |
+| `bg_kill` | SIGTERM the process group, then SIGKILL after `timeoutSec` (default 5). Runs `cleanup` if configured |
+| `bg_watch` | Change wake rules on a running process (`null` removes a pattern; `exit: false` / `output: false` silence those wakes) |
+| `bash` | Overrides the built-in bash tool. Quiet commands that stay running for `stalledSec` seconds (default 30) become managed background processes |
+
+Process records include `id`, `name`, `command`, `cwd`, `pid`, `state` (`running` \| `exited` \| `killed`), `exitCode`, `startedAt`, `endedAt`, `logLines`, and `backgrounded` (true only when `bash` moved the command).
+
+## Waking the agent
+
+A process delivers a custom message with `customType: "background-process"` and details `{ id, name, reasons, exitCode, matchedLine }`. `reasons` is a non-empty list drawn from `ready`, `error`, `output`, and `exit`.
+
+- Exit wakes by default. `ready` / `error` wake only when those regexes were supplied. `output` applies only to a command that `bash` auto-backgrounded: the first complete line after the switch.
+- `ready` and `output` fire at most once per process. `error` fires at most once per delivery window. `exit` fires exactly once when the process leaves `running`.
+- While the agent is streaming, the wake is queued as steering (after the current tool batch). While idle, it starts a new turn immediately.
+- `bg_kill` still delivers the single exit wake (`state: "killed"`). Silenced reasons never wake. After the exit wake, nothing else does.
+
+## Lifecycle
+
+Processes belong to the running pi process, not to a session. `/reload`, `/new`, `/fork`, and tree navigation do not stop them. Any session in the same pi process can list, read, and kill them.
+
+On pi exit (including `SIGTERM` / `SIGINT` that terminate pi), every managed process group is stopped with SIGTERM then SIGKILL, including children the command spawned. Logs and final records are stored under `SessionManager.getSessionDir()/background-processes/<pi-pid>/`. A later pi process that resumes the same session can `bg_list` those finished records and `bg_logs` their output; they are never reported as `running`.
